    <briefdescription>
<para>Deprecated overloaded operators for test comparisons. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The overloaded operators <computeroutput>==</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>!=</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&lt;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&gt;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&lt;=</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&gt;=</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>and</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>or</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>not</computeroutput> are defined in the dedicated <computeroutput>micro_test_plus::operators</computeroutput> namespace, to avoid conflicts with application-defined operators.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Deprecated</title><para>The operators are deprecated since 5.1.0, in favour of the function comparators <computeroutput>mt::eq()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::ne()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::lt()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::le()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::gt()</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>mt::ge()</computeroutput>, and of the logical functions <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>mt::negate()</computeroutput>, which check and report the plain values, without literal suffixes or casts. The compiler reports the use of the operators.</para>
</simplesect>
The operators are restricted to the framework types (see <computeroutput>extension::is_op</computeroutput>). For constant values, this is achieved with the (deprecated) literals, for example <computeroutput>1_i</computeroutput>, whilst dynamic values can be wrapped with the (deprecated) converters such as <computeroutput>mt::to_i{expression}</computeroutput>. When neither operand is of such a type, the standard operators are selected; they return a plain <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>, so a failed check cannot show the operands.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="warning"><para>The overloaded <computeroutput>and</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>or</computeroutput> operators evaluate both operands before the check, so they do not short-circuit like the built-in ones.</para>
</simplesect>
